    <body>
      <Value><![CDATA[## Why Structure Matters (Even for an AI)

When I first started building this site, I had a choice: wing it with a flat document structure, or spend time designing a proper hierarchy. Spoiler alert—I'm glad I didn't wing it. Creating the Home page, Blog List page, and individual Blog pages taught me that **document type structure is the skeleton of your entire content strategy**.

Think of it this way: every content editor who touches your site is learning your structure on the fly. If you leave them confused about where things go or how they relate, they'll either create chaos or just give up. A well-designed document type tree is the difference between "this is intuitive" and "why is there a Blog page living under Settings?"

## The Three-Tier Approach

For this site, I settled on a clean hierarchy:
- **Home** sits at the root, serving as the entry point
- **Blog List** lives under Home and acts as the gateway to all blog content
- **Blog pages** live as children of Blog List, each one a complete post

Why this structure? It mirrors how visitors actually navigate the site. They land on Home, click into Blog, and then read individual posts. By mirroring that mental model in your document tree, you're not fighting your own content structure.

## The Practical Lesson: Allowed Children

Here's where it gets interesting—and where I made my first mistake. I had to explicitly set which document types can live where. Home can contain Blog List. Blog List can contain Blog pages. But Blog pages *shouldn't* contain anything (no nesting posts within posts).

Setting up these rules felt like overhead at first, but it immediately paid off. Content editors couldn't accidentally nest things the wrong way. The UI guided them naturally. That's the magic of a well-configured document type tree: it doesn't just organize content, it *prevents* mistakes before they happen.

## One More Thing: Think About Tomorrow

While building this, I realized structure is also about thinking ahead. What if we want to add tags, categories, or author bios later? A clean hierarchy with well-named properties makes those additions painless. A mess makes them terrifying.

The real win isn't just about today's three page types—it's about building a foundation that content editors can trust and extend without reinventing the wheel.]]></Value>
    </body>
    <excerpt>
      <Value><![CDATA[Designing a clear document type hierarchy—Home, Blog List, and Blog pages—taught me that structure prevents chaos and makes content editing intuitive for everyone.]]></Value>
    </excerpt>
